<title>USA: U.S. Jan consumer prices rose 0.1 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Jan consumer prices rose 0.1 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-02-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly Consumer Price Index (CPI-U), 1982-84 equals 100:</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:     Seasonally Adjusted   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Jan    Dec--(Prev)   Jan97/96</p>
<p> All Items		    0.1    0.3    0.3     3.0</p>
<p> -Excluding Food/Energy   0.1    0.2    0.1     2.5</p>
<p> Energy			 0.8    1.5    1.6     7.9</p>
<p> Food, Drink		 -0.2   Unch    0.1     3.6</p>
<p> Food			  -0.3   Unch    0.1     3.6</p>
<p> CPI-Urban Consumers-X  159.1  158.6		N/A</p>
<p>X-Data unadjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes: Season.Adj.    Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Jan	 Jan97/96</p>
<p> Food at Home		-0.6	     3.9</p>
<p> -Beef and Veal	    -0.5	     1.9</p>
<p> -Pork			  0.3	    11.7</p>
<p> -Poultry (Unadjusted)    0.3	     6.9</p>
<p> -Fresh Fruits	     -5.3	     4.9</p>
<p> -Fresh Vegetables	 -0.1	    -1.7</p>
<p> --Tomato (Unadjusted)    0.1	     8.7</p>
<p> --Lettuce (Unadjusted)   6.1	    -2.8</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:     Seasonally Adjusted   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Jan    Dec--(Prev)   Jan97/96</p>
<p> Services		     0.2    0.3    0.2     3.3</p>
<p> Housing			0.3    0.2    0.3     3.0</p>
<p> -Home Fuel Oil	    -1.2    2.9    3.1    14.2</p>
<p> -Homeowner Costs-Y	 0.2    0.2    0.2     2.8</p>
<p> Apparel, Upkeep	    0.4    0.1    0.2    -0.3</p>
<p> Transportation	    -0.2    0.6    0.6     3.6</p>
<p> -Gasoline		    0.7    2.5    3.1     9.9</p>
<p> -New Cars		   -0.2    0.1    0.1     1.3</p>
<p>Y-Dec 1982=100 base.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:     Seasonally Adjusted   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Jan    Dec--(Prev)   Jan97/96</p>
<p> Medical Care		 0.2    0.2    0.2     2.9</p>
<p> Commodities		 Unch    0.2    0.3     2.7</p>
<p> CPI-W			  0.1    0.3    0.3     3.0</p>
<p>CPI-W--Index for urban wage earners and clerical workers.  </p>
<p>The 0.1 percent rise in the January CPI-U is the smallest monthly increase since a 0.1 percent rise in June 1996, the department said.</p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected a 0.3 percent rise in January consumer prices. Excluding food and energy, they expected a 0.2 percent increase.</p>
<p>The January report reflects the department's annual recalculation of seasonal adjustment factors.</p>
